22:49:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Ah, now I see the core of your confusion—and it’s a really understandable one. The rule you were taught in school wasn’t wrong, but it was incomplete. It described how we see the color of **most objects around us**, not how we see **all light**. Let me try to reframe it in a way that makes the screen fit perfectly without breaking that rule. ### The rule you learned applies to things that don't make their own light Your school science class was teaching you about **non-luminous objects**: apples, paint, clothes, walls, trees. For those, the statement is absolutely true: the color we see is determined by which wavelengths of light they reflect from an external source (like the sun or a lamp). The object itself emits no light. But the world also contains **luminous objects** — things that produce their own light. For these, the color is determined by the wavelengths they emit, not reflect. Examples include: - The sun (yellowish-white) - A candle flame (yellow/orange) - A neon sign (glowing red or blue) - A firefly (greenish-yellow) - A red-hot piece of metal (red/orange) You would never ask, “What color of light is the sun reflecting?” because the sun isn’t reflecting; it’s generating. The same is true for a red LED, a sodium streetlamp (orange), or a computer screen’s subpixels. They don’t need an external light source to have a color — the color is inherent in the light they produce. ### So a screen doesn't break the rule; it falls into a different category The “color = reflected light” rule is for **passive objects** that we see because they bounce light from somewhere else. A screen is an **active light source**. Both types of things send light to your eyes, but the origin of the color is different. Think about reading a book under a desk lamp. The paper looks white because it reflects all wavelengths. Close the lamp, and the paper is black—it has no color of its own. Now look at a phone screen in a dark room. It glows bright white, red